00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:28.560
If you’ve taken a biology class, at some point&nbsp;
you likely had to sit down to a picture of a cell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.720
It was vaguely oval shaped and filled with&nbsp;
circles and squiggly lines. And your task&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:41.120
was to look at those circles and lines and&nbsp;
assign them labels like “vesicle” or “nucleus.”&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:48.240
And this exercise has its benefits. But at the&nbsp;
same time, the picture it paints of a cell is—by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:56.640
necessity—static and uniform. The reality is&nbsp;
that cells may be full of analogous parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.880
but their lives and physical appearance can appear&nbsp;
vastly different when compared to each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:08.640
The bodies of multicellular organisms like&nbsp;
us are built on that principle, with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:16.560
diversity of cells dedicated to different tasks.
But we can see it even more starkly in the lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:23.520
of single-celled eukaryotes. These are organisms&nbsp;
who have managed to make an entire way of life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:29.280
out of a single cell, taking on many&nbsp;
different forms to do so. Even as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.720
inner parts remain approximately the same,&nbsp;
the final results can be wildly different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.840
whether you’re looking at the flexible form of&nbsp;
an amoeba or the determined shape of a diatom.&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.840
Here on Journey to the Microcosmos,&nbsp;
we often focus on the organisms—on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.880
their fascinating physiology and lives as&nbsp;
they navigate the world around them. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:58.800
what about the even smaller world inside of them?
If you think of a cell as its own little world, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:06.480
little society built on chemistry and biology, one&nbsp;
of its most important tasks is establishing its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:13.040
boundaries—separating and protecting the inside of&nbsp;
the cell while still leaving it just open enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:18.400
to bring in essential nutrients from the outside.
The essential component here is the plasma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:24.160
membrane, a double layer of fatty acids that&nbsp;
surrounds the cell and allows some things to pass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.240
while keeping the cell protected.
But for different protists, the cell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:34.800
membrane may not be quite enough to establish&nbsp;
the kind of boundaries it needs. Ciliates like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:40.640
these pelagotrix encase their plasma membrane&nbsp;
within a larger structure called the pellicle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:46.800
which has structures to define the organism’s&nbsp;
shape and anchor their many hairy cilia.&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:52.080
Other organisms supplement their plasma membrane&nbsp;
with another exterior wall, like this colony of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:58.000
green algae called Pediastrum. Each of these&nbsp;
cells is encased in a protective cell wall.&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:05.120
What comes next after the boundaries have&nbsp;
been set is, well, everything. It is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:12.880
big soup of life, organelles and nutrients&nbsp;
swimming in the watery broth of the cytoplasm.&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:19.360
And just like a good soup, there is a delicate&nbsp;
balance to the cytoplasm. When you’re living in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.600
a watery environment with a thin membrane&nbsp;
separating you from the outside world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:29.840
water can seep through. A little bit of that water&nbsp;
might be fine, but too much can be dangerous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:35.760
We’ve even seen microbes burst open and die when&nbsp;
they’ve been pushed past their watery limits.&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:42.880
But microbes are not passive in the face of this&nbsp;
danger. If you look at the edge of this Frontonia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:49.920
vernalis, you can see its solution at work.&nbsp;
There are canals all pointed towards the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:57.200
clear, circular region. That area is not just&nbsp;
some blank space. It is a contractile vacuole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:02.720
swelling up with water until it is full.&nbsp;
And when it is full, you can see it contract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:08.800
and push the water out of the cell through&nbsp;
tiny pores, leaving the world within balanced.&nbsp;

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:16.160
And as we travel further inside this world, there&nbsp;
is a flurry of activity, not all of which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:21.360
visible to us. Much of that activity is powered&nbsp;
by the mitochondria, though unfortunately they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:28.240
are a bit too small for us to easily see. But&nbsp;
we think that the thin rods inside this diatom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:34.800
might be the famous powerhouses of the cell.
The chemicals that mitochondria use to generate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:42.240
power come from food. And of course, microbes&nbsp;
have found many creative ways to obtain food.&nbsp;

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:48.720
Some organisms make their own, turning light into&nbsp;
sugar through clever chemistry and chloroplasts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.000
And even though chloroplasts can&nbsp;
come in different shapes and sizes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.120
they’re usually easy enough to identify through&nbsp;
their most obvious trait: their vibrant green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.680
color, derived from the light-absorbing&nbsp;
chlorophyll that powers the chloroplast.&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:08.320
Except…of course biology loves an exception.&nbsp;
There are examples of other colors in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:14.640
chloroplast realm, like in red algae that have&nbsp;
other pigments that stain their chloroplasts red.&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:20.880
Whatever the color, these are organisms&nbsp;
whose inner worlds can sustain themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:26.000
as long as they get enough light. But other&nbsp;
organisms have to go out and seek their sustenance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:31.680
by finding other organisms to eat. And if that&nbsp;
sounds like a bit much to you, I will remind you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.920
you are one of them. So, humans and other&nbsp;
organisms like us have to find a way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:43.840
introduce that food into their bodies.
In some organisms, that gate is called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:49.440
the cytostome. And in this glaucoma,&nbsp;
the cytostome takes the form of an oval,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:54.000
flapping as the organism turns&nbsp;
around and assesses a possible meal.&nbsp;

00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:00.160
But swallowing food is only one step in the&nbsp;
process. After it enters the organism’s body, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:06.000
food gets wrapped up in its own little membrane&nbsp;
like a present—a present stuffed with digestive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:12.320
enzymes that will tear its contents apart.
And just as with water, the cell doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:17.280
necessarily want to keep all of this digested food&nbsp;
around for the rest of its life. So what doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:24.160
get used gets pooped out through the cytoproct,&nbsp;
and if the mitochondria is the powerhouse of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:30.240
cell, the cytoproct is the butthole of the cell.
And then, of course, there are other specialized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:35.600
parts that help different organisms manage the&nbsp;
specific challenges of their lifestyle. Like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:41.520
take this Loxodes, which prefers to live in&nbsp;
areas of water that are lower in oxygen—areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.720
that are, to put it simply, down. Down&nbsp;
the water column, into the depths.&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:53.600
But to get there, the Loxodes needs to figure out&nbsp;
just where “down” is. So the organism relies on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.600 --> 00:07:00.080
its Muller vesicles, a simple structure, it’s&nbsp;
an organelle that allows it to detect gravity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:06.480
The vesicle holds a single mineral granule&nbsp;
attached to a cilium. And as gravity pulls on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:12.400
mineral, the mineral pulls on the cilium, pointing&nbsp;
the Loxodes downward like an inner compass.&nbsp;

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:17.840
Now if we take a step back and survey&nbsp;
these little worlds inside of organisms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:25.600
it is remarkable to realize that these organisms&nbsp;
have built themselves, assembled and sustained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:31.600
by their own reactions. They have gathered&nbsp;
and made their resources, and from it comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:37.680
all of these different parts that just seem to&nbsp;
work. There are so many bits of chemistry and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:43.840
probability that have come together to make&nbsp;
each of these tiny remarkable lives happen.&nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:50.720
And at the center of so much of that assembly&nbsp;
is the nucleus, those round organelles that hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:58.400
the DNA that describes the organism’s existence.
Nuclei are universal among eukaryotes, though of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:04.320
course ciliates in all of their contrarian glory&nbsp;
have also managed to make their own habits out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:11.680
of familiar entities. Ciliates are not content to&nbsp;
just have one type of nucleus, no they have two.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:16.880
The larger nucleus is called the macronucleus, and&nbsp;
it is responsible for the day-to-day genetic needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:23.040
of the organism. Whereas the smaller nuclei are&nbsp;
the micronuclei, and their primary role is to pass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:29.360
on genetic information during sexual reproduction.
It feels right to end with the nucleus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:34.880
the part of the cell that ensures not just the&nbsp;
life of this organism itself, but also of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:42.560
progeny. It is a map that is passed on and shared,&nbsp;
promising the creation of new worlds to come.&nbsp;

